    "description" : "This rule checks for and disallows nested method definitions inside other methods.",
    "explanation" : "\nChecks for nested method definitions.\n\n# Examples\n\n```ruby\n\n# bad\n\n# `bar` definition actually produces methods in the same scope\n# as the outer `foo` method. Furthermore, the `bar` method\n# will be redefined every time `foo` is invoked.\ndef foo\n  def bar\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  bar = -> { puts 'hello' }\n  bar.call\nend\n\n# good\n\n# `class_eval`, `instance_eval`, `module_eval`, `class_exec`, `instance_exec`, and\n# `module_exec` blocks are allowed by default.\n\ndef foo\n  self.class.class_eval do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n\ndef foo\n  self.class.module_exec do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n\n# good\n\ndef foo\n  class << self\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend# bad\ndef do_something\n  has_many :articles do\n    def find_or_create_by_name(name)\n    end\n  end\nend# bad\ndef do_something\n  has_many :articles do\n    def find_or_create_by_name(name)\n    end\n  end\nend# bad\ndef foo(obj)\n  obj.do_baz do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend# good\ndef foo(obj)\n  obj.do_baz do\n    def bar\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n[Source](http://www.rubydoc.info/gems/rubocop/RuboCop/Cop/Lint/NestedMethodDefinition)",
