    "description" : "Detects inefficient searching of keys and values within hashes using methods that allocate unnecessary arrays and perform slower searches.",
    "explanation" : "\nChecks for inefficient searching of keys and values within\nhashes.\n\n`Hash#keys.include?` is less efficient than `Hash#key?` because\nthe former allocates a new array and then performs an O(n) search\nthrough that array, while `Hash#key?` does not allocate any array and\nperforms a faster O(1) search for the key.\n\n`Hash#values.include?` is less efficient than `Hash#value?`. While they\nboth perform an O(n) search through all of the values, calling `values`\nallocates a new array while using `value?` does not.\n\n# Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.keys.include?(:a)\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.keys.include?(:z)\nh = { a: 1, b: 2 }; h.keys.include?(100)\n\n# good\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.key?(:a)\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.has_key?(:z)\nh = { a: 1, b: 2 }; h.key?(100)\n\n# bad\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.values.include?(2)\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.values.include?('garbage')\nh = { a: 1, b: 2 }; h.values.include?(nil)\n\n# good\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.value?(2)\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }.has_value?('garbage')\nh = { a: 1, b: 2 }; h.value?(nil)\n```\n\n[Source](http://www.rubydoc.info/gems/rubocop/RuboCop/Cop/Performance/InefficientHashSearch)",
