    "description" : "This rule enforces defining class methods using either `def self.method_name` or `class << self` syntax consistently in Ruby classes.",
    "explanation" : "\nEnforces using `def self.method_name` or `class << self` to define class methods.\n\n# Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\nclass SomeClass\n  class << self\n    attr_accessor :class_accessor\n\n    def class_method\n      # ...\n    end\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass SomeClass\n  def self.class_method\n    # ...\n  end\n\n  class << self\n    attr_accessor :class_accessor\n  end\nend\n\n# good - contains private method\nclass SomeClass\n  class << self\n    attr_accessor :class_accessor\n\n    private\n\n    def private_class_method\n      # ...\n    end\n  end\nend# bad\nclass SomeClass\n  def self.class_method\n    # ...\n  end\nend\n\n# good\nclass SomeClass\n  class << self\n    def class_method\n      # ...\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\n\n[Source](http://www.rubydoc.info/gems/rubocop/RuboCop/Cop/Style/ClassMethodsDefinitions)",
