    "description" : "This rule encourages using the Ruby method `transform_values` when transforming hash values instead of more verbose or less efficient alternatives like `each_with_object({})`, `map` followed by `to_h`, or `Hash[]` with `map`.",
    "explanation" : "\nLooks for uses of `+_.each_with_object({}) {...}+`,\n`+_.map {...}.to_h+`, and `+Hash[_.map {...}]+` that are actually just\ntransforming the values of a hash, and tries to use a simpler & faster\ncall to `transform_values` instead.\n\n# Examples\n\n```ruby\n# bad\n{a: 1, b: 2}.each_with_object({}) { |(k, v), h| h[k] = foo(v) }\nHash[{a: 1, b: 2}.collect { |k, v| [k, foo(v)] }]\n{a: 1, b: 2}.map { |k, v| [k, v * v] }.to_h\n{a: 1, b: 2}.to_h { |k, v| [k, v * v] }\n\n# good\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_values { |v| foo(v) }\n{a: 1, b: 2}.transform_values { |v| v * v }\n```\n\n[Source](http://www.rubydoc.info/gems/rubocop/RuboCop/Cop/Style/HashTransformValues)",
